# Directed acyclic graph utility implementation plan

Source proposal: `docs/directed-acyclic-graph-proposal.md`

## Goal

Implement and qualify the small domain-neutral `DirectedAcyclicGraph` utility
required by the verified-command resource index and cleanup planner. Keep this
work independent of DwarfSpec command, cleanup, ownership, journal, and service
policy so the prerequisite can be implemented and reviewed quickly.

## Scope guard

- [x] Treat `docs/directed-acyclic-graph-proposal.md` as the authoritative API
      and behavior contract.
- [x] Do not add node payloads, implicit node creation, persistence,
      serialization, concurrency, transitive reduction, visualization, resource
      semantics, cleanup semantics, or other consumer-specific behavior.
- [x] Keep node IDs as validated nonempty strings and keep the graph's internal
      representation private.
- [x] Add language-standard documentation comments for the class and every
      public method.

## Phase 1: Implement the graph contract

Outcome: `dwarfspec.graphs.directed_acyclic_graph` exposes the complete,
minimal, deterministic graph API without domain policy.

- [x] Add `src/dwarfspec/graphs/directed_acyclic_graph.lua` exporting one
      `DirectedAcyclicGraph` class-like table.
  - [x] Implement `new()` with private node, incoming-edge, and outgoing-edge
        storage suitable for straightforward adjacency-map operations.
  - [x] Implement shared nonempty-string node-ID validation used by every
        public method that accepts a node ID.
  - [x] Implement `add_node(node_id)`, returning `true` only for a new node.
  - [x] Implement `remove_node(node_id)`, atomically removing all incident
        edges and returning `true` only when the node existed.
  - [x] Implement `has_node(node_id)`.
  - [x] Implement `add_edge(predecessor_id, successor_id)` with validation for
        known endpoints, self-edges, duplicates, and direct or indirect cycles.
  - [x] Ensure a rejected edge insertion leaves all graph state unchanged.
  - [x] Implement `remove_edge(predecessor_id, successor_id)`, returning
        `false` for an absent edge only after validating both endpoints.
  - [x] Implement `has_edge(predecessor_id, successor_id)`.
  - [x] Implement `predecessors(node_id)` and `successors(node_id)` as fresh,
        lexically sorted arrays.
  - [x] Implement `topological_order(node_ids, less)` with Kahn's algorithm
        over the full graph or the selected induced subgraph.
  - [x] Validate the optional subset for duplicate and unknown node IDs.
  - [x] Validate that the optional comparator is callable.
  - [x] Document that a supplied comparator is caller-owned policy and must
        define a deterministic strict weak order.
  - [x] Select only among simultaneously ready nodes, use the supplied strict
        ordering when decisive, and use lexical node-ID ordering as the
        mandatory equivalence fallback.
  - [x] Return fresh arrays so callers cannot mutate graph state through query
        results.
- [x] Keep implementation complexity aligned with the proposal.
  - [x] Use expected O(1) node and edge membership.
  - [x] Accept O(V + E) node removal and cycle detection.
  - [x] Accept O(degree log degree) sorted-neighbor queries.
  - [x] Accept O(V^2 + E) topological ordering with a simple sorted ready list.
  - [x] Do not add caches whose invalidation would complicate the small graph.

Completion criteria:

- [x] The module implements exactly the proposal's public API and contains no
      DwarfSpec domain policy.
- [x] Duplicate additions and absent removals follow the specified idempotent
      boolean contract.
- [x] Invalid IDs, endpoints, subsets, comparators, self-edges, and cycles raise
      descriptive programming errors.
- [x] All returned collections are detached from internal state.

## Phase 2: Add exhaustive focused unit coverage

Outcome: one pure-Lua suite proves every API, validation, ordering, and
non-mutation guarantee without requiring DFHack or filesystem fixtures.

- [x] Add `tests/unit/graphs/directed_acyclic_graph_spec.lua`.
- [x] Cover construction and node behavior.
  - [x] Empty construction and empty full-graph ordering.
  - [x] Node insertion, duplicate insertion, membership, removal, and duplicate
        removal.
  - [x] Node removal clears every incoming and outgoing edge.
- [x] Cover edge behavior.
  - [x] Edge insertion, duplicate insertion, membership, removal, and duplicate
        removal.
  - [x] Unknown endpoints and self-edges are rejected.
  - [x] Direct and indirect cycles are rejected.
  - [x] Rejected edge insertions do not mutate nodes, edges, neighbors, or
        topological order.
  - [x] Cover input validation.
  - [x] Every public node-ID parameter rejects nil, non-string, and empty IDs.
  - [x] Neighbor queries and edge operations reject unknown IDs.
  - [x] A supplied non-function comparator is rejected.
  - [x] Duplicate and unknown induced-subset IDs are rejected.
- [x] Cover neighbor results.
  - [x] Direct predecessor and successor queries are lexically sorted.
  - [x] Mutating returned neighbor arrays does not mutate graph state.
- [x] Cover deterministic topological ordering.
  - [x] Chains, forks, diamonds, and disconnected nodes preserve every edge.
  - [x] Simultaneously ready nodes use lexical order by default.
  - [x] A custom comparator controls simultaneously ready nodes.
  - [x] A deterministic strict-weak-order comparator produces stable repeated
        results for the same ready-node set.
  - [x] Lexical fallback resolves comparator equivalence deterministically.
  - [x] A comparator cannot violate predecessor-before-successor edges.
  - [x] Induced subsets ignore edges crossing the subset boundary while
        preserving edges internal to the subset.
  - [x] Mutating a returned order does not mutate graph state.

Completion criteria:

- [x] The focused suite covers every case listed by the source proposal.
- [x] The suite uses only in-memory Lua values and runs without a DFHack
      process, scheduler, service, cleanup registry, or filesystem fixture.
- [x] The focused suite completes in well under one second under the ordinary
      unit-test harness.

## Phase 3: Qualify and review the prerequisite

Outcome: the standalone utility has sufficient focused evidence to unblock the
verified-command implementation without running unrelated exhaustive suites.

- [x] Run the focused directed-acyclic-graph unit suite.
- [x] Run the repository's ordinary Lua syntax and formatting checks for the
      new module and suite.
- [x] Run the relevant source declaration checks for the new public class and
      methods.
- [x] Run `git diff --check` for the planning and implementation diff.
- [x] Perform a follow-up implementation review against every API, boundary,
      ordering, complexity, and unit-test requirement in the source proposal.
- [x] Confirm the API exposes the generic operations required by the planned
      `ResourceDependencyIndex` and `CleanupPlanner` without exposing domain
      methods.
- [x] Record focused command output and review findings before marking this
      prerequisite complete.

Validation record (2026-08-06): `Run-UnitTests.ps1` selected the graph suite
with 13 successes, 0 failures, 0 errors, and 0 pending in 0.015 seconds.
`Check-Lua.ps1` passed for 376 files; valid and invalid source-declaration
checks passed; and `git diff --check` passed. Independent review found the
exact generic API, required active-claim and temporary transaction-graph
operations, no domain-policy leakage, and no missing proposal behavior.

Completion criteria:

- [x] The focused unit suite and applicable static/declaration checks pass.
- [x] Follow-up review finds no missing proposal behavior or unsupported scope.
- [x] No full unit suite, live automation, service validation, or consumer
      integration proof is required for this standalone prerequisite.

## Final acceptance

- [x] Every requirement in `docs/directed-acyclic-graph-proposal.md` is
      implemented and verified.
- [x] `DirectedAcyclicGraph` remains small, deterministic, domain-neutral, and
      safe for both active-claim and temporary transaction graphs.
- [x] The verified-command plan may treat this checklist as a completed
      prerequisite rather than duplicating graph implementation work.
